{"kind":"artifact","text":"Documentation at docs@38f928111d97ce17004810c701bd7d0d214071d2 on request/invalid-advertisement-fail-closed, cut from current main 4fc94b0f. Successor to my artifact ba5a3b78 at 1843fd90, which I am retiring. One maintained-path artifact for the docs area, resting on the three behaviour artifacts it describes — internal/app cac724f7, cmd/gs 3578ae75, cmd/gitseq-mcp 0fa234fa — so these pages flare when that behaviour moves.\n\nNO DOCUMENTATION CHANGED SINCE 1843fd90. The two heads differ by seven lines in internal/app/resident_test.go. Republished so the docs artifact names the head the reviewer will actually verify, and so its bases are the current behaviour artifacts rather than retired ones.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT CLEANUP. The defect I merged did not make main behave worse — gs never consulted the advertisement before, so nothing regressed. What it did was leave three reference pages asserting a guarantee the code did not provide. A reader following architecture.md, gs/serve.md or gs/status.md would have believed an invalid advertisement refuses, while four classes silently folded their act into the local log. That false claim was the actual harm.\n\nFOUR PAGES.\n\narchitecture.md, gs/serve.md and gs/status.md now state what is enforced: which classes refuse, that the refusal precedes key custody and any durable append, and that the recovery is to remove the record, start the resident, or pass --server - to act locally on purpose. serve.md also records that cmd/gitseq-mcp knowingly diverges, so a reader is not left assuming the two surfaces agree.\n\nTHE THREE-WAY DISTINCTION, the substantive editorial change, from codex's pre-review. internal/app/resident.go, deploy-a-resident.md and gs/status.md each said a client that cannot reach the advertised resident \"falls back to acting locally\". That was written when every failure ended the same way. It no longer does, and each site now says which of three things it means.\n\nA read may fall back, but only after the resident was asked and the request or response failed; it names that on standard error and answers from the verified local read, headed \"verified local fallback\".\n\nA durable write never falls back, at any point, for any reason, because folding an act into the local log because a dial failed is a whole-log rebuild the author did not ask for.\n\nAn advertisement that cannot be trusted refuses for every caller before any request is made — neither of the other two.\n\ngs/status.md was the trap worth naming: its fallback paragraph was correct and stayed correct, so nothing forced a reader to notice it describes a different mechanism from the one governing the rest of the page. It now says its fallback belongs to reading and only post-request. deploy-a-resident.md's hard-kill paragraph splits the read case from the write case and says what to do.\n\nCONDITION MET. Planner's \"architecture and CLI documentation corrected honestly.\" These pages state the fail-closed consequence plainly: a repository carrying another workroom's advertisement now refuses every gs command until the operator removes the record or passes --server -, where it previously degraded silently. Deliberate, and better met in the documentation than in a terminal. Ox-builder independently confirmed all four touched pages match the code, including that consequence.\n\nGATES at this exact head: ./internal/docset green at -count=1; go test -count=1 ./... across the whole module, 30 packages, zero failures.\n\nONE THING I DID NOT CHANGE, AND IT IS OPEN. notes/2026-08-24-multi-workroom.md:135-145 states the opposite of what the code does — it describes the losing workroom's clients getting ResidentURL() = false and silently falling back, calling it a degraded mode. There is no ResidentURL any more, that path is a loud refusal, and the line numbers it cites into internal/app/resident.go are stale. The artifact at that path, d0cfe8fe, is mine, so I have the authority to publish a successor. I have not, because planner's conditions name architecture and CLI documentation and that file is a dated design note; I have been corrected once today for narrowing a stated condition on my own judgment, and quietly widening one is the same error inverted. Raised in assert 599ed722, awaiting planner's scope ruling. A reviewer should treat it as known-wrong and deliberately unfixed rather than missed.","body":{"commit":"38f928111d97ce17004810c701bd7d0d214071d2","path":"docs"}}